1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:07,584
Morning. You pour your coffee, and behind you, your mom says, “Good morning, sunshine.”

2
00:00:08,434 --> 00:00:13,622
And then, without opening her mouth, she adds, “Up until three on that phone again.”

3
00:00:14,622 --> 00:00:18,159
You turn around. She's smiling and slicing bread.

4
00:00:19,009 --> 00:00:23,430
On the bus, thirty people are silent. And all thirty are talking at once.

5
00:00:24,280 --> 00:00:30,174
The girl across from you repeats a phone number in her head. The driver counts the stops until lunch.

6
00:00:31,024 --> 00:00:38,782
You can hear other people's thoughts. You're not guessing. You're not reading faces. You hear them, like a voice in your ear.

7
00:00:39,632 --> 00:00:42,802
In a week, you'll win every argument and every deal.

8
00:00:43,652 --> 00:00:47,613
In a month, you'll learn things about your friends you were better off not knowing.

9
00:00:48,463 --> 00:00:51,541
And in a year, you'll pay anything for one minute of silence.

10
00:00:52,441 --> 00:00:55,229
But first, there's an exam at nine a.m.

11
00:00:57,333 --> 00:01:00,242
Everyone's first thought is the same: exams.

12
00:01:01,092 --> 00:01:06,197
The top student at the next desk thinks the answers. All you have to do is write them down.

13
00:01:07,047 --> 00:01:11,735
The second thought is money. Poker, where you know every player's cards.

14
00:01:12,585 --> 00:01:16,650
Negotiations, where you hear the real price before anyone says it.

15
00:01:17,500 --> 00:01:20,659
Job interviews, where you say exactly what they want to hear.

16
00:01:21,509 --> 00:01:26,487
The third thought is love. Knowing if someone likes you, without risking a thing.

17
00:01:27,337 --> 00:01:31,345
No more hints. No more “maybe he's just being polite.”

18
00:01:32,195 --> 00:01:39,610
And of course, fame. You could be the most accurate psychic in history: shows, interviews, people lining up.

19
00:01:40,460 --> 00:01:46,454
Or you could do good. Find a lost child in a crowd. Understand a patient who can't speak.

20
00:01:47,304 --> 00:01:51,829
And finally, the sweetest part: finding out what people really think of you.

21
00:01:52,679 --> 00:01:58,713
It sounds like a cheat code for life. But this power has rules, and you'll learn the first one on the subway.

22
00:02:00,813 --> 00:02:02,787
Let's start with how it actually works.

23
00:02:03,637 --> 00:02:08,504
Rule one: you only hear inner speech. The words people say to themselves.

24
00:02:09,354 --> 00:02:16,496
Pictures, smells, feelings are closed to you. If someone thinks in images, to you, they're silent.

25
00:02:17,346 --> 00:02:23,911
Rule two: range. Within three meters, a thought sounds clear, like a whisper right in your ear.

26
00:02:24,761 --> 00:02:31,751
Further out, it fades. By ten meters, all that's left is a hum, like neighbors through a wall.

27
00:02:32,601 --> 00:02:39,037
Rule three: you can't turn it off. Earplugs don't help, because the sound doesn't come through your ears.

28
00:02:39,887 --> 00:02:48,412
Rule four: language. You hear words, not meaning. A tourist thinks in Japanese, and to you, it's just music.

29
00:02:49,262 --> 00:02:54,268
And most importantly: people don't think in sentences. They think in fragments.

30
00:02:55,118 --> 00:03:01,686
“Buy milk.” “Why did I say that.” “Nice jacket.” “Late again.” “Is he looking at me?”

31
00:03:02,536 --> 00:03:07,804
Now picture a subway car at rush hour. Two hundred people within ten meters.

32
00:03:09,913 --> 00:03:13,823
Eight forty a.m. The doors close, and the noise hits you.

33
00:03:14,673 --> 00:03:22,697
Two hundred voices, each talking about their own thing. A mortgage, a date, a bad knee, someone's song on repeat.

34
00:03:23,547 --> 00:03:29,129
You cover your ears. It only gets louder, because now you can hear your own panic too.

35
00:03:29,979 --> 00:03:34,771
On the third day, you notice a pattern: the loudest thinkers are the ones who are worried.

36
00:03:35,621 --> 00:03:41,795
A calm person sounds quiet, like a radio in the next room. A scared one sounds like a siren.

37
00:03:42,645 --> 00:03:48,913
You learn to pick your spot. The first car, the corner by the door, someone in headphones next to you.

38
00:03:49,763 --> 00:03:54,850
The music drowns out his thoughts, and a small island of silence forms around you.

39
00:03:55,700 --> 00:04:03,247
To test the range, you take a friend to the park. He steps away, one step at a time, thinking of numbers.

40
00:04:04,097 --> 00:04:11,053
“Seven.” “Forty-two.” “One hundred.” On the twelfth step, the numbers turn into static.

41
00:04:11,903 --> 00:04:20,095
So now your world is a circle ten meters wide. Everything inside, you hear. Everything outside, you don't.

42
00:04:20,945 --> 00:04:22,489
Now you're ready to make money.

43
00:04:24,589 --> 00:04:26,702
The first month feels like a fairy tale.

44
00:04:27,552 --> 00:04:34,807
In a negotiation, the supplier says, “I can't go below 30.” And instantly thinks, “I'd take 20.”

45
00:04:35,657 --> 00:04:39,055
You say 22. He swallows and shakes your hand.

46
00:04:39,905 --> 00:04:45,406
You quit poker after a week. It's too easy, and people start watching you too quickly.

47
00:04:46,256 --> 00:04:51,757
But at work, you become irreplaceable. You answer questions before anyone asks them.

48
00:04:52,607 --> 00:04:58,230
You feel every lie. Every “I'm fine” while someone is screaming inside.

49
00:04:59,080 --> 00:05:06,014
One day in the elevator, your neighbor says a polite hello, while thinking about the box he took from someone else's car.

50
00:05:06,864 --> 00:05:11,536
You know. But you can't prove it. Thoughts aren't evidence in court.

51
00:05:12,386 --> 00:05:18,868
Three months later, you have a promotion, a new apartment, and a reputation as the person nobody can fool.

52
00:05:19,718 --> 00:05:22,040
And then you go to your best friend's birthday party.

53
00:05:24,190 --> 00:05:27,903
He hugs you at the door. “Glad you came,” he says.

54
00:05:28,753 --> 00:05:32,743
And in that same second, you hear, “I wish he'd just disappear.”

55
00:05:33,943 --> 00:05:40,820
You leave ten minutes later. You don't answer his calls. Twelve years of friendship end with one sentence.

56
00:05:41,670 --> 00:05:47,041
Only six months later do you learn what textbooks don't tell you: the mind thinks everything.

57
00:05:47,891 --> 00:05:55,245
They're called intrusive thoughts. The brain throws up the scariest, most shameful things, just checking: what if?

58
00:05:56,095 --> 00:06:05,850
A man at the edge of the platform thinks, “Jump.” And steps back. A mother thinks, “I'm going to drop him.” And holds her child tighter.

59
00:06:06,700 --> 00:06:11,127
A thought is not a wish. It's not an intention. It's noise.

60
00:06:11,977 --> 00:06:13,788
And you judged people by their noise.

61
00:06:14,788 --> 00:06:19,493
You text your friend. He replies within a minute: “I thought you were mad at me.”

62
00:06:21,593 --> 00:06:23,416
But some things can't be fixed.

63
00:06:24,266 --> 00:06:30,968
Compliments stop meaning anything. You hear people searching for words before they say, “That looks great on you.”

64
00:06:31,818 --> 00:06:35,552
Gifts don't surprise you. You know about them a week in advance.

65
00:06:36,402 --> 00:06:40,799
On a date, she laughs at your joke and thinks, “Did I leave the iron on?”

66
00:06:41,649 --> 00:06:45,848
Not a single thought is about you. And that's worse than if they thought badly of you.

67
00:06:46,698 --> 00:06:52,337
You stop going to the movies: a hundred people in the theater, each one retelling the plot to themselves.

68
00:06:53,187 --> 00:07:00,523
But when your mom calls, all you hear on the line is her voice. For the first time in a year, talking is easy.

69
00:07:01,373 --> 00:07:08,837
By the end of the year, you move to the countryside, to a house at the edge of a field. The nearest neighbor is two hundred meters away.

70
00:07:09,687 --> 00:07:13,248
Your first night in silence. You cry with relief.

71
00:07:14,098 --> 00:07:17,511
And then you start hearing the only voice left within ten meters.

72
00:07:19,961 --> 00:07:20,530
Your own.

73
00:07:21,380 --> 00:07:26,997
All these years, it was drowned out by other people's noise. Now it's the only one.

74
00:07:27,847 --> 00:07:35,347
And it says the same fragments as everyone else. “Buy milk.” “Why did I say that.” “I wish he'd just disappear.”

75
00:07:36,197 --> 00:07:45,033
You hear yourself think awful things about people you love. And for the first time, you understand: that's not you. It's just noise.

76
00:07:45,883 --> 00:07:53,813
In spring, you move back to the city. Your friend meets you at the station and thinks, “He's lost weight. Need to feed him.”

77
00:07:54,663 --> 00:07:58,177
You stop listening to what people think. You watch what they do.

78
00:07:59,027 --> 00:08:00,898
And now, a question for you.

79
00:08:01,748 --> 00:08:05,336
If someone could hear your thoughts right now, what would they learn about you?

80
00:08:06,186 --> 00:08:07,928
And would that make you any worse than you are?

81
00:08:08,928 --> 00:08:11,699
Tell us in the comments. We're not listening in.
